## 16. llama.cpp SYCL request-to-output (DeepSeek-V4 K216)

Full walk, buses, and ranked levers: `research/dsv4-k216-request-to-output-pipeline-20260825.md`. Sister notes: `research/dsv4-k216-prefill-ubatch-20260825.md`, `research/dsv4-k216-dspark-and-public-numbers-20260825.md`, `research/dsv4-k216-buun-moe-cache-20260825.md`.

C1 HTTP on this host is: socket → JSON/chat template → tokenize → ubatch graph (`min(-b,-ub)`) → serial `-sm layer` GPU0 then host-staged copy then GPU1 → DSA/indexer attention (not spec decode) → routed MoE `MUL_MAT_ID` → sample → chunked socket. Localhost HTTP/tokenize/sample are not the wall.

**Decode wall (PROVISIONAL best 8.87 engine tok/s, L5 `-ncmoe 15 -ts 65,35`):** IQ3_XXS is omitted from fused SYCL `MUL_MAT_ID` MMVQ, so each routed projection D2H-copies ~24 B of expert IDs and `stream->wait()` (~84 waits/token on L5 GPU-routed layers), then host-launches tiny GEMVs. `-ncmoe N` puts the first N layers’ **experts** on CPU (mmap + DDR4-2133). Dual split is capacity, not per-layer 2×; no P2P (both GPUs on CPU-attached x8 slots, measured H2D ≈ 8.9/7.9 GB/s).

**Prefill wall (PROVISIONAL best ~105 pp at `-b/-ub 1024`):** host counting-sort grouping + IQ3→FP16 then oneMKL. ub512→1024 already ~1.26–1.30×. Remaining ~2× is a grouped IQ3 kernel, not another flag sweep.

Do not retry: `-ts 46,54`/`50,50` low ncmoe, `-sm row`, single GPU, GPU-resident Unsloth DSpark (256-expert `dflash`) on L3/L5, `ggml_sycl_supports_mmq()` flip, SYCL graphs on dual+`MUL_MAT_ID`, `--no-mmap`/`--mlock` on this ~83 GiB GGUF, q4_0 K, spiritbuun `-ot 'exps=CPU' --moe-cache on` on this SYCL build (CUDA-only cache; all-CPU experts without it is slower than L5). CPU-MoE DSpark accepted well and was slower (2.48 tg). Packed P15_65 loaded (GPU0 1897 / GPU1 1720 MiB free) then exited unmeasured; P15_60 SYCL1 OOM 2.21 GiB.

Architect range with fused IQ3 MMVQ and a **compatible 216-expert** GPU draft: ~9.6–18 tok/s. **30 tok/s C1 is not realistic on this SYCL IQ3 MoE path.** Label all of the above provisional; no public headline.

## 17. Megakernels, Persistent Fusion & Xe2/XMX Guidelines

Full reference manual: `docs/reference/megakernel-comprehensive-reference.md`; architecture guide: `research/megakernel-xe2-xmx-architecture-guide-20260829.md`; roadmap: `research/megakernel-implementation-roadmap-20260829.md`; policy: `decisions/adr-0009-xe2-megakernel-and-persistent-fusion-policy.md`.

### Core Rules for Kernel Optimization on B70:
1. **Decode ($M=1$) vs. Prefill Separation:** In single-token decode, standard multi-kernel execution wastes $500+\,\mu\text{s}$ per token in host-driver launch latency and repeatedly writes $8\text{ KB}$ activations to GDDR6. Persistent kernels maintain the residual stream in on-chip GRF (registers) / SLM across all layers.
2. **Single-Card Persistent Grid:** Target 32 work-groups (1 per Xe-core) on a single B70. Never attempt cross-card Tensor-Parallel (TP) megakernels across PCIe (x8 + x8, CPU-attached, no P2P) (`ADR-0008`); use independent single-card persistent pipelines instead (`ZE_AFFINITY_MASK`).
3. **Native DPAS Formats Only:** Persistent megakernels and XMX fast-paths require native systolic formats: **INT4 (GPTQ/AWQ)**, **INT8**, or **FP16/BF16**. Non-native formats (IQ3, Q3_K) force scalar unpack loops on Vector Engines and destroy persistent performance.
4. **Sub-Group Specialization:** In Xe-core work-groups, dedicate 1–2 producer sub-groups to asynchronous 2D block prefetching (`esimd::block_load_2d`) from GDDR6 to SLM, and 6–7 consumer sub-groups to XMX `dpas` systolic compute.
5. **Host-Sync Elimination Gate:** Any MoE/GEMV optimization must verify zero intermediate host synchronizations (`stream->wait()` or D2H expert-ID copies). On-device router-to-GEMV dispatch is mandatory.
6. **vLLM Decode Kernel Bounds:** Before benchmarking vLLM on Xe2, ensure `vllm-xpu-kernels` is built with explicit sub-group size bounds (`[[intel::reqd_sub_group_size(16)]]`) and `VLLM_PAGED_DECODE_CONFIG` to prevent GRF register spills (`research/xe2-esimd-launch-bounds-2026-08.md`).

## 18. Architecture-First Triage & Diagnostic Quick Reference

Authority: `docs/methods/architecture-diagnostic-playbook.md`.

Before modifying configs, writing kernels, or launching sweeps, determine the model class and hardware placement:

| Model Family / Setup | Dominant Bottleneck | Primary Lever & Action | Never Attempt (Anti-Pattern) |
|---|---|---|---|
| **Large Dense ($27\text{B}\text{–}70\text{B}$)**<br>e.g. `Qwen3.8-27B` | GDDR6 Memory Bandwidth ($31\text{ ms}$ base) | 1. Speculative Decode (MTP4 / DFlash)<br>2. Block-FP8 W8A16 linear reroute (`patch_fp8_w8a16.py`)<br>3. Native Xe2 small-$M$ GEMM (`research/qwen38-fp8-tp2-speed-attack-and-ceilings-20260829.md`) | Base GEMV megakernels (saves $<1.5\%$); tuning host flags for base decode |
| **Pure MoE**<br>e.g. `DeepSeek-V4 K216`, `Ornith-1.5` | Host-Device Sync ($84+$ D2H waits & `stream->wait()`) | 1. Keep router `top_k_indices` on-device<br>2. Fused `mul_mat_id_fused_moe_sycl`<br>3. Level Zero graph capture (`research/dsv4-k216-request-to-output-pipeline-20260825.md`) | `-sm row` (segfaults); CPU-MoE without cache (`spiritbuun -ot exps=CPU` is slower); sweeping low `-ncmoe` |
| **Hybrid SSM + MoE**<br>e.g. `Nemotron-3.5-30B` | Sequential Dispatch Lag ($736$ launches per token) | 1. Fused Mamba2 SSD block kernel in GRF/SLM<br>2. On-device 128-expert MoE router<br>3. DFlash drafter (`models/nemotron/nemotron-3.5-lightning-30b-a3b/serving.md`) | Unfused Mamba2 layers on XPU; older Q4_0 GGUF routes |
| **Dual B70 (X570-F)**<br>No GPU P2P (x8 + x8, CPU-attached) | Inter-Card CCL Traffic | 1. Default to independent single-card engines (`ZE_AFFINITY_MASK`) (`ADR-0008`)<br>2. If TP2, require worker affinity + SYS_PTRACE + host-staged CCL | Stock Docker TP2 without affinity patches; expecting $2\times$ scaling over the x8 links; inter-card TP megakernels |


